In order to adjust the Michaelis-Menten model, we will have to process the given data in order to obtain $1/r$ and $1/[S]$ as shown in the table below, where $1/r$ are expressed in s/mM and $1/[S]$ in $\times 10^{-3}$ mM$^{-1}$.
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{   c|cccccccc       }
\toprule
  $1/r$   &  0.39& 0.17&0.07&0.04  &0.02&0.01&0.01&$9.49\times 10^{-3}$ 
 \\
  \midrule
$1/[S]$  &20 &10& $4$&2  &1& 0.4& 0.2& 0.125\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center} 
Now, we can adjust a line to the data, knowing that $1/r$ would be the OY axis and $1/[S]$ the OX axis. The corresponding equations would be: 
\[\frac{1}{r}=18.92\cdot \frac{1}{[S]}+7.34 \times 10^{-4}\] 
We have that $\frac{K_m}{r_m}$ is 18.92s and $\frac{1}{r_m}$ is $7.34\times 10^{-4}$s/mM, with a $r^2$ value of 0.99. The graph follows a nice straight line, as shown in the image below. Therefore $r_m$ is 1363 s/mM and $K_m$ is 25776mM.
